<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Armaztsikhe</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Armazi fortress</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Armaztsikhe is the ruined acropolis of ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberia</a> on the heights of Bagineti, above the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kura-river">Mtkvari</a> river opposite <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mtskheta">Mtskheta</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a>. Excavations since the 1930s have revealed fortification walls, a palace complex, baths and rich burials of the Hellenistic and Roman periods. Georgian chronicles describe it as the seat of the early kings and of the idol of the god Armazi.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/armaztsikhe.jpg" alt="Armaztsikhe" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: AFCP · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:AFCP_on_Location_in_the_Republic_of_Georgia._Armaztsikhe_03.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
